    {
      "id": "question",
      "number": 2,
      "title": "The Record",
      "summary": "Agent Fox examines declassified paperwork while a vintage map and archive labels drift behind him.",
      "duration": 33,
      "authoredDuration": 14,
      "transition": "ink",
      "narration": "Let's start with what the CIA itself told the Senate. According to the CIA director's testimony, MKUltra ran from 1953 to 1964. That's eleven years. The surviving records list 149 subprojects. They name 80 institutions, and 44 of them were colleges and universities. They also name 185 researchers and assistants from outside the government.",
      "keywords": [
        "1953",
        "1964",
        "eleven years",
        "149 subprojects",
        "80 institutions",
        "44",
        "185"
      ],
      "sourceIds": [
        "senate-1977"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "experiments",
      "number": 3,
      "title": "The Experiments",
      "summary": "A laboratory sketch: a glass vial, paperwork and a reel-to-reel recorder animate into view.",
      "duration": 34,
      "authoredDuration": 14,
      "transition": "flash",
      "narration": "So what were all these subprojects actually doing? The director sorted them into categories. Among them: drugs and alcohol. Hypnosis. Sleep research. Polygraphs. The record also covers drugs meant as aids to interrogation. LSD was among the drugs tested. And here is the part that matters most. Some of the people tested were volunteers. Some were never told.",
      "keywords": [
        "categories",
        "LSD",
        "interrogation",
        "volunteers",
        "never told"
      ],
      "sourceIds": [
        "senate-1977"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cost",
      "number": 4,
      "title": "The Human Cost",
      "summary": "Evidence of testing without informed consent. The room turns quiet and serious.",
      "duration": 40,
      "authoredDuration": 18,
      "transition": "fade",
      "narration": "The testimony counted six subprojects that tested drugs on unwitting people. Unwitting means they didn't know. Some of those tests happened in safe houses in San Francisco and New York City. There was no informed consent. One case shows what that meant. Dr. Frank Olson was an Army civilian employee. In 1953, at a meeting called by the CIA, he was given LSD without knowing it. About a week later, he was dead.",
      "keywords": [
        "six subprojects",
        "unwitting",
        "safe houses",
        "no informed consent",
        "Frank Olson"
      ],
      "sourceIds": [
        "senate-1977"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "revelation",
      "number": 5,
      "title": "The Revelation",
      "summary": "Committee and hearing papers fill an evidence board. Agent Fox points to the records.",
      "duration": 46,
      "authoredDuration": 18,
      "transition": "ink",
      "narration": "So how did any of this come out? In January 1975, the Senate created a special committee, chaired by Senator Frank Church. Its job was to find out whether America's intelligence agencies had done anything illegal, improper or unethical. Then, in 1977, a CIA employee found seven boxes of MKUltra records at a retired records center. That August, senators questioned the CIA director at a joint hearing. Senator Edward Kennedy said the CIA had drugged American citizens without their knowledge or consent.",
      "keywords": [
        "Frank Church",
        "illegal, improper or unethical",
        "seven boxes",
        "joint hearing",
        "Edward Kennedy"
      ],
      "sourceIds": [
        "senate-history-church",
        "church-1976",
        "senate-1977"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "known",
      "number": 6,
      "title": "What We Know",
      "summary": "The room brightens. Agent Fox closes the folder and a source-based takeaway appears.",
      "duration": 46,
      "authoredDuration": 17,
      "transition": "iris",
      "narration": "But those boxes were never the full story. A 1963 report by the CIA's Inspector General led to the end of unwitting testing in 1964. And in 1973, MKUltra files were destroyed on the authority of the CIA director at the time. Senator Kennedy named him: Richard Helms. What survived was mostly finance folders. Advances, vouchers, accountings. So what do we actually know? Enough to show it happened. Not enough to know everything that happened. Much of the full story may never be known.",
      "keywords": [
        "1963",
        "1964",
        "1973",
        "destroyed",
        "Richard Helms",
        "finance folders",
        "never be known"
      ],
      "sourceIds": [
        "senate-1977"
      ]
    }
